1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:04,455
From above, a Korean apartment
complex can look like a

2
00:00:04,455 --> 00:00:08,909
city assembled from the same
building again and again.

3
00:00:08,909 --> 00:00:15,096
Inside, that repeated skyline can
contain a whole network of everyday life.

4
00:00:15,546 --> 00:00:19,221
Walk between the towers
and the picture changes.

5
00:00:19,221 --> 00:00:23,356
A playground, a walking path,
a shared entrance, and a

6
00:00:23,356 --> 00:00:28,410
maintenance office can all belong
to the same residential complex.

7
00:00:28,860 --> 00:00:32,926
Once several buildings share
grounds, facilities, and

8
00:00:32,926 --> 00:00:37,530
management, the layout starts
to organize more than housing.

9
00:00:37,530 --> 00:00:39,985
It organizes movement, spending,

10
00:00:39,985 --> 00:00:43,668
maintenance, and the
boundary of a neighborhood.

11
00:00:44,118 --> 00:00:47,673
The official census for 2025 counted

12
00:00:47,673 --> 00:00:52,118
apartments as 65.8% of
Korea's housing stock.

13
00:00:52,118 --> 00:00:55,278
The figure counts housing units.

14
00:00:55,728 --> 00:00:58,965
To understand the system,
start at 3 scales.

15
00:00:58,965 --> 00:01:04,335
There is a private home behind a front
door, a building around that home,

16
00:01:04,335 --> 00:01:09,264
and a complex that connects several
buildings through shared space.

17
00:01:09,714 --> 00:01:15,050
Furniture, meals, storage, and
sleep happen within private rooms.

18
00:01:15,050 --> 00:01:19,484
Just outside that door,
everyday life begins to depend

19
00:01:19,484 --> 00:01:23,178
on equipment and space
shared with neighbors.

20
00:01:23,628 --> 00:01:27,066
An elevator links many
homes to the ground.

21
00:01:27,066 --> 00:01:31,383
A roof or a pipe can serve
several households at once.

22
00:01:31,383 --> 00:01:36,420
Each private home relies on
systems extending beyond its walls.

23
00:01:36,870 --> 00:01:41,891
At the next scale, the complex
groups those buildings together.

24
00:01:41,891 --> 00:01:43,883
The Korean term is danji.

25
00:01:43,883 --> 00:01:48,426
Its grounds can bring multiple
entrances, parking spaces,

26
00:01:48,426 --> 00:01:53,526
landscaping, and resident facilities
into a single managed area.

27
00:01:53,976 --> 00:01:59,414
Picture an illustrative site with
several towers, a central play area,

28
00:01:59,414 --> 00:02:04,308
an entrance for vehicles, and
walking routes between buildings.

29
00:02:04,308 --> 00:02:07,104
Its entrances shape everyday routes.

30
00:02:07,554 --> 00:02:11,946
Building upward can fit more
homes on a given footprint.

31
00:02:11,946 --> 00:02:15,239
It can become landscaping, a playground, a

32
00:02:15,239 --> 00:02:18,690
road, or an entrance
to underground parking.

33
00:02:19,140 --> 00:02:23,177
Some complexes move much of
their parking below ground.

34
00:02:23,177 --> 00:02:26,994
This makes room for a quieter
surface layout, though

35
00:02:26,994 --> 00:02:30,737
vehicle access, loading,
and emergency routes still

36
00:02:30,737 --> 00:02:34,260
have to fit somewhere
within the overall design.

37
00:02:34,710 --> 00:02:38,600
A direct path to a play area
makes a trip convenient.

38
00:02:38,600 --> 00:02:43,076
Repeating that arrangement across
several buildings turns the

39
00:02:43,076 --> 00:02:47,406
shared grounds into part of
daily household infrastructure.

40
00:02:47,856 --> 00:02:53,929
Korea's housing construction rules
explicitly recognize resident facilities.

41
00:02:53,929 --> 00:02:57,605
Their categories include
playgrounds, exercise

42
00:02:57,605 --> 00:03:01,440
spaces, reading rooms,
and childcare facilities.

43
00:03:01,440 --> 00:03:05,036
What a particular complex
contains depends on

44
00:03:05,036 --> 00:03:08,472
its size, age, and
applicable requirements.

45
00:03:08,922 --> 00:03:13,976
A reading space or exercise room
places an activity close to home.

46
00:03:13,976 --> 00:03:17,804
The complex can combine
residential buildings with

47
00:03:17,804 --> 00:03:21,786
facilities that households
use on a recurring basis.

48
00:03:22,236 --> 00:03:25,949
The same arrangement raises
a question about access.

49
00:03:25,949 --> 00:03:30,091
A facility managed for residents
has a defined user group.

50
00:03:30,091 --> 00:03:33,804
Access from outside depends
on the facility’s rules.

51
00:03:34,254 --> 00:03:40,057
Elevators require servicing, lights need
replacing, and grounds need cleaning.

52
00:03:40,057 --> 00:03:43,480
The management office
provides a visible point

53
00:03:43,480 --> 00:03:46,902
where those ordinary
tasks can be coordinated.

54
00:03:47,352 --> 00:03:52,353
For complexes covered by the relevant
management rules, a residents'

55
00:03:52,353 --> 00:03:57,132
representative council helps decide
important management matters.

56
00:03:57,132 --> 00:04:02,280
Its decisions can concern budgets,
common facilities, and maintenance.

57
00:04:02,730 --> 00:04:06,964
Cleaning, maintenance, and
operation require resources.

58
00:04:06,964 --> 00:04:11,120
The convenience of having
many services on the grounds

59
00:04:11,120 --> 00:04:15,354
comes with a continuing need
to fund and organize them.

60
00:04:15,804 --> 00:04:18,213
A repair can affect several homes.

61
00:04:18,213 --> 00:04:22,110
Deciding when to replace
something may involve both its

62
00:04:22,110 --> 00:04:26,148
present condition and the cost
that residents will share.

63
00:04:26,598 --> 00:04:29,778
Similar layouts and
building forms can make it

64
00:04:29,778 --> 00:04:32,682
easier to recognize what is being offered.

65
00:04:32,682 --> 00:04:37,590
A floor plan can be compared with
another unit using familiar features.

66
00:04:38,040 --> 00:04:41,580
That comparison can include
the size of the unit,

67
00:04:41,580 --> 00:04:44,543
its floor, and the complex it belongs to.

68
00:04:44,543 --> 00:04:48,444
An apartment becomes a home
that can also be described

69
00:04:48,444 --> 00:04:51,768
through a fairly consistent
set of attributes.

70
00:04:52,218 --> 00:04:57,500
The national real transaction price
system publishes reported apartment

71
00:04:57,500 --> 00:05:02,188
sales alongside information such
as floor area and floor level.

72
00:05:02,188 --> 00:05:05,833
Those records make actual
transactions visible to

73
00:05:05,833 --> 00:05:09,330
someone looking at a
particular housing market.

74
00:05:09,780 --> 00:05:12,397
The same apartment now has 2 roles.

75
00:05:12,397 --> 00:05:15,612
It provides rooms for
living, and ownership

76
00:05:15,612 --> 00:05:18,603
gives someone an asset that can be sold.

77
00:05:18,603 --> 00:05:23,388
Daily comfort and a future sale
can enter the same conversation.

78
00:05:23,838 --> 00:05:27,440
The person living there may
own the unit or rent it.

79
00:05:27,440 --> 00:05:31,457
Ownership and occupancy can
sit with different households.

80
00:05:31,457 --> 00:05:34,089
That distinction matters when a shared

81
00:05:34,089 --> 00:05:37,206
decision affects both
the home and its owner.

82
00:05:37,656 --> 00:05:40,287
For an owner, a shared repair can be an

83
00:05:40,287 --> 00:05:43,188
expense needed to keep
the building useful.

84
00:05:43,188 --> 00:05:47,506
It can also be considered through
the apartment's marketability.

85
00:05:47,506 --> 00:05:52,296
These concerns can pull attention
toward the same maintenance decision.

86
00:05:52,746 --> 00:05:55,672
The rooms are lived in today while the

87
00:05:55,672 --> 00:05:59,369
property can be priced
for a future transaction.

88
00:05:59,369 --> 00:06:03,450
The 2 time horizons exist
in the same physical space.

89
00:06:03,900 --> 00:06:06,801
The design also comes with tradeoffs.

90
00:06:06,801 --> 00:06:08,133
Follow 3 of them.

91
00:06:08,133 --> 00:06:14,561
Repeated forms can narrow visual variety,
asset considerations can enter household

92
00:06:14,561 --> 00:06:20,676
decisions, and a clear perimeter can
separate the complex from nearby streets.

93
00:06:21,126 --> 00:06:23,333
Start with visual uniformity.

94
00:06:23,333 --> 00:06:29,041
Repeated windows and towers can give
a development a recognizable identity.

95
00:06:29,041 --> 00:06:33,532
Across a large site, the same
repetition can make different

96
00:06:33,532 --> 00:06:38,022
corners look similar, especially
when seen from the street.

97
00:06:38,472 --> 00:06:41,070
The interiors can still be different.

98
00:06:41,070 --> 00:06:45,072
Households furnish and use
their rooms in their own ways.

99
00:06:45,072 --> 00:06:48,372
The visual tradeoff
appears at the scale of the

100
00:06:48,372 --> 00:06:51,672
buildings and the
streetscape surrounding them.

101
00:06:52,122 --> 00:06:55,952
A complex built and managed
as a connected system

102
00:06:55,952 --> 00:06:59,313
can also make piecemeal
change complicated.

103
00:06:59,313 --> 00:07:04,082
Altering an entrance, a common
area, or a shared facility can

104
00:07:04,082 --> 00:07:08,850
require coordination beyond a
single household's preferences.

105
00:07:09,300 --> 00:07:11,008
Now move to the edge.

106
00:07:11,008 --> 00:07:16,133
The inside may prioritize
residential paths and shared grounds.

107
00:07:16,133 --> 00:07:18,979
The outside street may carry shops,

108
00:07:18,979 --> 00:07:22,640
traffic, and routes used
by many more people.

109
00:07:22,640 --> 00:07:25,812
These spaces meet at specific openings.

110
00:07:26,262 --> 00:07:30,743
A boundary becomes especially
important when paths stop at it.

111
00:07:30,743 --> 00:07:35,079
A short straight line on a map
can become a longer walk if a

112
00:07:35,079 --> 00:07:39,126
pedestrian has to go around
a site to reach an entrance.

113
00:07:39,576 --> 00:07:44,125
Imagine a destination directly
across an illustrative block.

114
00:07:44,125 --> 00:07:48,446
A passage through the middle
would connect the two sides.

115
00:07:48,446 --> 00:07:50,872
A perimeter route adds distance.

116
00:07:50,872 --> 00:07:53,828
This example shows how access rules can

117
00:07:53,828 --> 00:07:57,240
change a walk without
moving its destination.

118
00:07:57,690 --> 00:08:01,671
Actual complexes differ in
how open their grounds are.

119
00:08:01,671 --> 00:08:05,577
Where are the entrances,
which paths connect through,

120
00:08:05,577 --> 00:08:09,042
and who can use each
facility once they arrive?

121
00:08:09,492 --> 00:08:15,019
An internally focused layout can draw
everyday movement toward the grounds.

122
00:08:15,019 --> 00:08:19,661
That can change how much activity
faces the surrounding street.

123
00:08:19,661 --> 00:08:25,188
The effect depends on the entrances,
the edge, and the destinations nearby.

124
00:08:25,638 --> 00:08:28,968
A sheltered residential
space and a connected

125
00:08:28,968 --> 00:08:32,371
city street serve different
kinds of movement.

126
00:08:32,371 --> 00:08:35,848
The layout has to
accommodate the people living

127
00:08:35,848 --> 00:08:39,030
inside while meeting
a wider urban network.

128
00:08:39,480 --> 00:08:44,705
Transit, schools, workplaces,
and shops sit beyond the complex.

129
00:08:44,705 --> 00:08:49,349
Even a development with many
internal facilities depends

130
00:08:49,349 --> 00:08:53,496
on connections that reach
outside its own grounds.

131
00:08:53,946 --> 00:08:58,568
A group of towers may look
self-contained from above, but its

132
00:08:58,568 --> 00:09:02,887
entrances feed into streets,
and those streets connect to

133
00:09:02,887 --> 00:09:06,978
destinations that the complex
cannot supply by itself.

134
00:09:07,428 --> 00:09:13,334
An apartment skyline becomes easier to
read when you ask about its ground level.

135
00:09:13,334 --> 00:09:17,838
Follow the paths, look at the
facilities, find the management

136
00:09:17,838 --> 00:09:21,972
boundary, and notice how the
edge meets the next street.

137
00:09:22,422 --> 00:09:25,889
The private home, the
shared building, and the

138
00:09:25,889 --> 00:09:28,903
managed complex keep operating together.

139
00:09:28,903 --> 00:09:32,746
Each scale supplies something
the others depend on.

140
00:09:32,746 --> 00:09:37,494
Each also introduces decisions
about cost, control, and access.

141
00:09:37,944 --> 00:09:43,221
This is how a repeated building
form becomes a whole housing system.

142
00:09:43,221 --> 00:09:49,119
It gathers homes, services, shared
obligations, and saleable property into a

143
00:09:49,119 --> 00:09:54,552
place that many households experience
as their immediate neighborhood.

144
00:09:55,002 --> 00:09:57,423
Return to the forest of towers.

145
00:09:57,423 --> 00:10:00,312
The repetition catches the eye first.

146
00:10:00,312 --> 00:10:05,466
The routes between buildings, the
facilities behind doors, and the

147
00:10:05,466 --> 00:10:11,322
obligations shared across homes explain
what makes that landscape function.

148
00:10:11,772 --> 00:10:15,552
The next time a Korean
apartment skyline appears on

149
00:10:15,552 --> 00:10:18,961
screen, look for the
space between the towers.

150
00:10:18,961 --> 00:10:24,445
That is where the complex starts to
reveal how it organizes everyday life.

151
00:10:24,445 --> 00:10:26,076
This is Only in Korea.
